WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:05.840
Welcome to another Stories From The
Stores

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:07.960
Now today we're joined by curator Alex Rose

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.380
and she's told me that this
instrument can measure ozone.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:13.400
Alex, how does it work?

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.360
Ozone is a gas that's produced up in the stratosphere,

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.220
so that's a layer of the
Earth's atmosphere that's between

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:24.360
10 and 50 kilometers up and ozone absorbs UV light from the Sun.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:26.800
Okay, like a layer of sun cream?

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.700
Basically like a layer of sun cream,

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:32.120
and that's the principle that this instrument uses to measure ozone.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.220
It was made in 1924 by a university professor

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.240
called Gordon Dobson, in his shed, and he realized that

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.820
no one had taken regular measurements of ozone and

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.340
he wanted to create a more continuous data set.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:48.720
So he constructed
this instrument to do that.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.300
It would have been mounted so that you could adjust the height,

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:55.840
there's a scale on the side where you can see what angle it's pointing at

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.200
so that you could angle it at the Sun or whatever angle you wanted.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.660
Is that why it's got a bubble meter?

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.060
Yeah, exactly so that he could calculate the height.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.400
It has a filter at this end that

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.160
separated out the wavelengths of light
that he was interested in recording.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.740
There's a prism here that splits
out the light, and then the readings

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:17.500
would have been taken on a photographic
plate that sat just down there.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.280
Okay so he's taking all of these readings,

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.620
did he find that hole in the ozone layer?

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:26.340
He didn't because in 1924 it didn't exist.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:30.240
What he did discover was that the levels of ozone weren't constant

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:35.480
they fluctuated a lot, so they varied daily,
they varied annually, seasonally.

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:38.960
So did they use this object to discover the
hole in the ozone layer?

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:42.960
It was a modern version of this instrument stationed at Antarctica

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:47.159
that a number of British scientists were looking at
measurements from and they realised that

00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:50.909
levels of ozone were much lower than
they were expecting and were declining.

00:01:50.909 --> 00:01:55.829
So after the initial findings in the mid
1980s there was a big international

00:01:55.829 --> 00:01:59.560
research project that confirmed the
findings that there was a hole in the ozone layer

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:06.280
and the world came together
to ban the use of CFC in the manufacturing industry

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.349
which was the cause of the hole.

00:02:08.349 --> 00:02:10.180
And that's all thanks to Dobson.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:14.920
Exactly, so scientists still use his name every time they take measurements

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:20.300
because the modern versions of this instrument are called Dobson meters by scientists

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.220
and the unit of measurement for atmospheric ozone

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.300
in fact bears his name, it's called the
Dobson!

